<Base title="Contribute" description="How Inferview lessons and problems are written and checked." active="none">
  <div class="wrap page narrow prose">
    <h1>Contribute</h1>
    <p>Lessons and problems are MDX files with checked frontmatter. The source repository is not public yet; this page will link to it when it is. Until then, here is the format so you can draft.</p>

    <h2>A problem</h2>
    <p>One file per problem at <code>src/content/problems/&lt;track&gt;/&lt;id&gt;.mdx</code>, where the id is a three-letter track code and a number, such as <code>agt-002</code>. The frontmatter sets the type (concept, estimate, debug, code, design, behavioural or mcq), difficulty, seniority and minutes, plus what the type needs: three to five choices with exactly one correct for mcq and debug, an accepted range for estimates, and starter code, tests and a solution for code problems. The body has the sections Question, Hints, Answer, Follow-ups and Common mistakes, in that order.</p>

    <h2>A lesson</h2>
    <p>One lesson per module at <code>src/content/lessons/&lt;track&gt;/&lt;module&gt;.mdx</code>: a labelled scenario, the idea with toy numbers, a widget or a prediction, real numbers with sources, short runnable code, one interview question answered at three depths, and the limits of the lesson's model of the world.</p>

    <h2>The rules that are checked</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Every number, quote or borrowed example has an entry in <code>sources</code> with a title and a URL.</li>
      <li>A company appears in <code>companies</code> only with a public URL that names the company and the topic.</li>
      <li>No em dashes, and no en dashes used as dashes.</li>
      <li>Track and module slugs must exist, the problem id must match its file name, and the lesson file name must match its module.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><code>npm run validate</code> checks all of this in a second, without a full build.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
